<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adams–Onís Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Transcontinental Treaty, Florida Purchase Treaty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Adams–Onís Treaty, signed in Washington on 22 February 1819 by the American secretary of state John Quincy Adams and the Spanish minister Luis de Onís, transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It also fixed the boundary between the United States and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> to the Pacific. The United States assumed up to five million dollars of its citizens' claims against Spain, and the treaty took effect in 1821.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adams-onis-treaty.jpg" alt="Adams–Onís Treaty" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adams_onis_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
